+
+I'll implement this by teaching the assertion rewriter to "unroll" `all()`/`any()` over comprehensions into a loop, so a failing `all` reports the first falsy element (just like the equivalent `for` loop) instead of showing `all(<generator object ...>)`.
+
+Let me start by looking at the relevant rewriting code.
+
+# Plan
+
+1. In `src/_pytest/assertion/rewrite.py`, detect calls of the form `all(<comp>)` / `any(<comp>)` where `<comp>` is a generator/list/set comprehension with a single, simple `for`-clause.
+2. Rewrite such a call into an unrolled `for` loop that short-circuits (preserving `all`/`any` semantics) but keeps the **first** falsy element's values available so the existing explanation machinery can produce a meaningful `+ where ...` line.
+3. Fall back to the normal handling for anything that isn't the simple, common case (e.g. multi-clause comprehensions, tuple targets, shadowed names is not detectable but that's the same heuristic used elsewhere).
+4. Add tests.
