+
+## How it works
+
+For `assert all(is_even(x) for x in [1, 3, 5])` the rewriter now emits the equivalent of:
+
+```
+@py_assert0 = True
+for x in [1, 3, 5]:
+    @py_assert2 = is_even(x)
+    if not @py_assert2:
+        @py_assert0 = False
+        break
+if not @py_assert0:
+    raise AssertionError(...)
+```
+
+The element expression is visited with the normal explanation machinery (by temporarily redirecting `self.statements` into the loop body, the same technique `visit_BoolOp` already uses), so the failure message reuses the existing call explanation and ends up as:
+
+```
+E       assert False
+E        +  where False = is_even(1)
+```
+
+which is exactly the third ("for loop") form from the report. Because the result variable plus `break` preserve `all`/`any` short-circuiting, runtime semantics are unchanged; the same unrolling applies to `any(...)`, list comprehensions, and comprehensions with `if` clauses (the `if` clauses are turned into nested `if` statements around the body).
+
+## Tests to run
+
+I can't execute them here, but the targeted commands are:
+
+```
+python -m pytest testing/test_assertrewrite_all_any.py -v
+python -m pytest testing/test_assertrewrite.py testing/test_assertion.py -v
+```
+
+The new tests assert that `all`/`any` failures now include a `where ... = is_even(...)` line (and no longer contain `generator object`), that short-circuiting is preserved, and that passing asserts and custom messages still behave. The existing `test_assertrewrite.py`/`test_assertion.py` suites exercise the modified `visit_Call_35`/`visit_Call_legacy`/`visit_Compare` code paths broadly and should confirm nothing else regressed.
+
+Two notes on deliberate limitations: the unrolled loop binds the comprehension's target name in the enclosing scope (so, like the hand-written `for`-loop form, the loop variable can leak), and we only unroll the single-clause, plain-name-target case — everything else keeps the previous behavior.
