+## How it works
+
+For `assert all(is_even(x) for x in [1, 3, 5])` the rewriter now emits the equivalent of initializing a result flag to `True`, running a `for` loop whose body evaluates `is_even(x)`, and on the first falsy element assigns `False` and `break`s. `any(...)` is symmetric with a `False` start and a truthy break. Because the result flag and `break` preserve short-circuiting, runtime behavior is unchanged.
+
+The element expression is visited with the normal explanation machinery, by temporarily redirecting `self.statements` into the loop body (the same technique `visit_BoolOp` already uses), so the failure message reuses the existing call explanation and ends up as an `assert False` line plus a `+  where False = is_even(1)` line — exactly the third ("for loop") form from the report. Comprehensions with `if` clauses are handled by wrapping the loop body in the corresponding nested `if` statements.
+
+## Tests to run
+
+I can't execute them from here, but the targeted commands are:
+
+- `python -m pytest testing/test_assertrewrite_all_any.py -v`
+- `python -m pytest testing/test_assertrewrite.py testing/test_assertion.py -v`
+
+The new tests assert that `all`/`any` failures now include a `where ... = is_even(...)` line (and no longer contain `generator object`), that short-circuiting is preserved, and that passing asserts and custom messages still behave. The existing `test_assertrewrite.py`/`test_assertion.py` suites exercise the modified `visit_Call_35` / `visit_Call_legacy` / `visit_Compare` code paths broadly and should confirm nothing else regressed.
+
+Two deliberate limitations: the unrolled loop binds the comprehension's target name in the enclosing scope (so, like the hand-written `for`-loop form, the loop variable can leak), and we only unroll the single-clause, non-async, plain-name-target case — everything else keeps the previous behavior.
